{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfriVH9igkzj3Cos73BS6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In this sitting I ruled LLR-069 and TC-069 MEANING and re-approved both, and I gave SR-236 its first approval. Each was done in its own commit, and the commit hooks passed on all three.\n\n**Verdict:** `docs/reviews/wi-853-findings-gate-in-rework/004-ADJUDICATE-1ccd09d.md`, commit `96d63770`, trailer `WI: WI-853`. It ends with `SITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment;first-approval`.\n\n**`## amendment`** (`VERDICT: MEANING rows=2`)\n- **LLR-069 (Detail): MEANING.** Citing an accepted DISMISS is no longer enough to clear a finding. The findings file beside that dispute verdict must also show the ruled finding with the same text as the excluded F#. Code that met the old text would accept a dismissal of an unrelated finding, or of another round's finding with the same number. The new text makes either one exit 1.\n- **TC-069 (Expected): MEANING.** The test must now check three new refusals: an unrelated finding, another round's same-numbered finding, and a finding that can't be shown.\n- I would approve both texts, and they match the code:\n  - `_correspondence_problem` refuses when the ruled finding doesn't match the F#.\n  - `_finding_key` ignores only list markers, severity tags, typographic punctuation and extra whitespace when comparing.\n  - `tests/test_plan_coverage.py` and `tests/test_plan_coverage_step.py`: 46 passed.\n- **Re-approval:** commit `9ee5c700`, recorded as act 78, run with `--reattests LLR-069,TC-069 --verdict <004>`. Only those two rows moved in the snapshot. Both stay `Approved`, and I edited no registry cell.\n\n**`## first-approval`** (`OUTCOME: APPROVE rows=1`)\n- **SR-236: APPROVE.** It is the parent requirement my dispute ruling 003 called for, and the chain holds up:\n  - **Upward:** it is labelled as a derived requirement under SN-024, names its deriving viewpoint (UNATTENDED-OPS), argues the alternative it rejected, and feeds back to SN-024. That is the same pattern as SR-234 and SR-235.\n  - **Sideways:** it doesn't overlap SR-154, SR-155, SR-234 or SR-235.\n  - **Downward:** LLR-069 implements every acceptance clause, and TC-069 tests each one, including that an uncovered finding refuses the dispatch.\n- **Approval:** commit `df474260`. SR-236's `Status` went from `Drafted` to `Approved` with no other cell touched, and the snapshot (`--approves \"docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml=WI-853\"`) added only SR-236. Nothing was refused.\n\n**Follow-up:** WI-805, the loop's replan, will use the same shared step but cites only SR-154 today. When it's built, it should cite SR-236 as well. I left this as a note in the verdict rather than a reason to return the row.\n\nThe coordinator's `004-ADJUDICATE-1ccd09d.md.requested` marker is still untracked; I left it alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":192,"cache_read_input_tokens":93459,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":192},"output_tokens":822,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1fb1008-6d93-4693-a49f-a0783f483182","uuid":"5c652d0e-6c1d-4585-8f46-f336d7f42060","timestamp":"2026-10-09T13:08:44.562Z","request_id":"req_011CfriVGrc1xK2RzXHZWTi4"}
